<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Data Examples</h2>

<div id="A2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We augment constraint descriptions to a ground-truth domain and problem file and create new ground-truth PDDL files that now encode the ground-truth. Here we include an example constraint description and the ground-truth PDDL files for the constraint + problem pair. Listings <a href="#LST5" title="Listing 5 ‣ Appendix F Example Model Outputs ‣ Language Model as Planner and Formalizer under Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, <a href="#LST6" title="Listing 6 ‣ Appendix F Example Model Outputs ‣ Language Model as Planner and Formalizer under Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> and <a href="#LST7" title="Listing 7 ‣ Appendix F Example Model Outputs ‣ Language Model as Planner and Formalizer under Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> display an example constraint description and the annotated ground-truth <math id="A2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{DF}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{DF}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{PF}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{PF}</annotation></semantics></math> for a paired BlocksWorld problem. Listings <a href="#LST8" title="Listing 8 ‣ Appendix F Example Model Outputs ‣ Language Model as Planner and Formalizer under Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, <a href="#LST9" title="Listing 9 ‣ Appendix F Example Model Outputs ‣ Language Model as Planner and Formalizer under Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a> and <a href="#LST10" title="Listing 10 ‣ Appendix F Example Model Outputs ‣ Language Model as Planner and Formalizer under Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> display an example Domain, Problem and Constraint Description as well as their groundtruth <math id="A2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{DF}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{DF}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{PF}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{PF}</annotation></semantics></math> in PDDL. Listing <a href="#LST11" title="Listing 11 ‣ Appendix F Example Model Outputs ‣ Language Model as Planner and Formalizer under Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> shows an example ground-truth <math id="A2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{PF}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{PF}</annotation></semantics></math> for the XL dataset, the <math id="A2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{DF}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{DF}</annotation></semantics></math> remains the same. Listings <a href="#LST12" title="Listing 12 ‣ Appendix F Example Model Outputs ‣ Language Model as Planner and Formalizer under Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>, <a href="#LST13" title="Listing 13 ‣ Appendix F Example Model Outputs ‣ Language Model as Planner and Formalizer under Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> and <a href="#LST14" title="Listing 14 ‣ Appendix F Example Model Outputs ‣ Language Model as Planner and Formalizer under Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> display the corresponding constraint description, <math id="A2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{DF}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{DF}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{PF}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{PF}</annotation></semantics></math> that have now been obfuscated.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Prompts</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Listings <a href="#LST15" title="Listing 15 ‣ Appendix F Example Model Outputs ‣ Language Model as Planner and Formalizer under Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>, <a href="#LST16" title="Listing 16 ‣ Appendix F Example Model Outputs ‣ Language Model as Planner and Formalizer under Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>, <a href="#LST17" title="Listing 17 ‣ Appendix F Example Model Outputs ‣ Language Model as Planner and Formalizer under Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>, <a href="#LST18" title="Listing 18 ‣ Appendix F Example Model Outputs ‣ Language Model as Planner and Formalizer under Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> and <a href="#LST19" title="Listing 19 ‣ Appendix F Example Model Outputs ‣ Language Model as Planner and Formalizer under Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a> displays the prompts for all experiment settings given to all models. Whenever possible, we asked the model to return the output in a JSON object for easier parsing.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix D </span>Experimental Setup Details</h2>

<section id="A4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.1 </span>Planner</h3>

<div id="A4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use a <span id="A4.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">dual-bfws-ffparser</span> planner to find plans from the generated PDDL. This is a best first width search planner used with a fast forward planner.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.2 </span>VAL</h3>

<div id="A4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The VAL library takes in a ground-truth PDDL <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{DF}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒟</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{DF}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{PF}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{PF}</annotation></semantics></math> and a plan and tries to execute the plan in the environment by checking whether each action in the found plan can be executed based on the preconditions written in the ground-truth files. If the plan is executable, VAL then checks whether the final state after executing the plan matches the goal state found in the ground-truth <math id="A4.SS2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{PF}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒫</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{PF}</annotation></semantics></math>. If either the plan is not executable or the final state does not match the goal state, VAL will return an error, therefore the plan found by the planner is not correct.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">D.3 </span>LTL</h3>

<div id="A4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Given problem description, the language model is asked to generate LTL formulas for the initial state, goal state, environment dynamics, and action dynamics. All formulas are concatenated into a conjunctive form as the formal representation for planning. When a constraint are introduced, we make a separate call to the language model for the formula representing the constraint and concatenate the formula to the existing conjunctive formula.
We employ the spot library <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Duret-Lutz et al. (2022)</a></cite> for encoding LTL formulas into Büchi automatons. Then, the state traces satisfying the problem specification can be found by searching for a Lasso-shaped accepting run.
To make the results compatible with our evaluation pipeline, we map the traces back to sequences of PDDL actions by inferring actions from abstract state changes. In this process, the language model is also asked to generate environmental information, which is the room connectivities in CoinCollector domain.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A5" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix E </span>Full Results</h2>

<div id="A5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Tables <a href="#A6.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Appendix F Example Model Outputs ‣ Language Model as Planner and Formalizer under Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> and <a href="#A6.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Appendix F Example Model Outputs ‣ Language Model as Planner and Formalizer under Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> display results for all methodologies on <span id="A5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">BlocksWorld-100</span> and <span id="A5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">CoinCollector-100</span>. Tables <a href="#A6.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Appendix F Example Model Outputs ‣ Language Model as Planner and Formalizer under Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> and <a href="#A6.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Appendix F Example Model Outputs ‣ Language Model as Planner and Formalizer under Constraints" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> display results for <span id="A5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">BlocksWorld-XL--100</span> and <span id="A5.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_markedasmath ltx_font_typewriter">MysteryBlocksWorld-100</span> on PDDL.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A6" class="ltx_appendix">
